// ============================================================ ch 3
= Chapter 3: Assessment Methodology

Chapter 3 states how the assessment was carried out. It covers retrieval and
cleaning, the construction of the demand surface and the candidate set, the
selection method and the guarantee it carries, and the validation applied before
findings were released.

== Retrieval and Cleaning

Each source is governed by a handbook file that declares its endpoint, the
meaning of its fields, and the cleaning rules to be applied in order. Each rule
reports its own drop count, so no record leaves the pipeline unaccounted for.

#let cleanrows = d.provenance.map(p => (
  p.source, thousands(p.rows_raw), thousands(p.rows_clean), thousands(p.dropped_total)))
#figure(
  gov-tbl(
    columns: (1fr, auto, auto, auto),
    align: (left, right, right, right),
    header: ([Source], [Returned], [Retained], [Discarded]),
    rows: cleanrows),
  caption: [Records Retrieved and Retained],
)

#let wp = d.provenance.find(p => p.dropped_total > 0)
#if wp != none [
  Records were discarded from #wp.source for the following reasons:
  #wp.drops.pairs().map(((r, n)) => thousands(n) + " " + r).join("; ").

  #callout("Why Discarded Records Are Itemised")[
    A register that returns a record does not necessarily hold a distinct, current
    observation. Where a source ships duplicate rows and superseded observations in
    the same response, coverage computed on the raw response counts settlements
    twice and treats retired observations as current. Every rule that removes a
    record is declared in advance and reports how many it removed, so the
    difference between the returned and retained counts above is fully explained.
  ]
]

== Coordinate Reference System

All distance, radius and separation computations are carried out in
#scope.crs. #scope.crs_rationale

Over #str(scope.crs_check.pairs) sampled pairs of demand cells, projected
distance departs from great-circle distance by
#pct(scope.crs_check.median_relative_error, digits: 3) at the median and
#pct(scope.crs_check.max_relative_error, digits: 3) at most, which on a
#str(calc.round(scope.radius_m)) m service radius is about
#str(calc.round(scope.crs_check.max_relative_error * scope.radius_m)) m.

#callout("Why Distance Is Not Computed in Degrees")[
  Computing distance directly from latitude and longitude is among the most
  common errors in applied spatial analysis, because a degree of longitude is
  not a fixed ground distance and shrinks towards the poles. A service radius is
  precisely the kind of claim that must hold in metres on the ground, so the
  analysis projects first and measures second. The figures above state how far
  the chosen projection departs from the sphere here, rather than assuming the
  difference away.
]

== Demand Surface

Population is taken from a gridded estimate at approximately 100 m resolution,
read over the area of interest and aggregated into blocks coarser than the source
and finer than the service radius, so that aggregation does not bias coverage.
#thousands(base.demand_cells) demand cells result, together holding
#thousands(base.population) people.

== Candidate Set

#thousands(base.candidates) candidate locations were evaluated. Two kinds were
admitted: an existing #scope.unit recorded as not serving, which could be
rehabilitated, and a regular lattice of new locations at
#str(calc.round(scope.grid_m)) m spacing. Lattice points already inside an
existing service area were removed before selection. Recommendations are
constrained to lie at least #str(calc.round(scope.min_separation_m)) m apart.

== Selection Method

#plan.at("method", default: "The selection method was not recorded for this run.")

#plan.at("guarantee", default: "")

#callout("Coverage Is a Union, Not a Sum")[
  A settlement within reach of two #scope.unit#if scope.budget > 1 [s] is served
  once, not twice. Coverage throughout this assessment is therefore the union
  over selected locations. Summing the population served by each location
  separately overstates coverage, and the independent recount reported at
  Section 5.2 states by how much it would have done so here.
]

== Validation

Before findings were released, the plan was checked by a separate process that
can reject it but cannot amend it, and which does not read the reasons recorded
in Chapter 5. A single rejection stops the assessment being issued. The results
of those checks are at Section 5.2.

